# Companion bots: several small bots that chat with each other in one channel (jokes, questions, fun facts).
# Copy to .env and fill in the tokens. Start with: docker compose up -d --build
#
# Create one application per bot in the Discord Developer Portal (Bot tab > Reset Token). Use 2 to 30; the more, the livelier.
# They do not need any privileged intent. Invite each one with this link (replace CLIENT_ID with that bot's Application ID):
#   https://discord.com/oauth2/authorize?client_id=CLIENT_ID&scope=bot%20applications.commands&permissions=562949954538560
# (that is View Channel, Send Messages, Read Message History and Send Polls). The FIRST token hosts the /companions command.
# Then run /companions setup in your server to choose the channel.
COMPANION_TOKENS=

# Time zone for quiet hours and the daily limit
COMPANION_TIMEZONE=Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh

# Optional: a read-only /status.json with totals only (no names, ids or messages). Off unless set.
# STATUS_PORT=8788
# STATUS_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Optional: Gemini API key for the AI features (a companion answers when mentioned, a daily riddle). Off for every server
# until a manager runs /companions ai replies or /companions ai daily. Only the single message that mentions a bot is sent to Google.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash
# AI_DAILY_LIMIT=60

# Optional: Discord user IDs (comma separated) of the owners of the bots. To an owner the companions act shy: they pretend not to care
# and secretly like them (welcome, voice greetings, goodbyes, reminders and AI replies). Everyone else gets the normal, warm lines.
# OWNER_IDS=

# Optional: a Discord webhook URL. The bots send a short message there when something breaks (a bot offline or a voice bot
# unable to rejoin for more than 10 minutes, an unhandled error) and when they start. Leave it empty for no alerts.
# ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=
